**Claim.** The Galois group of $g(x)$ is the full symmetric group $S_p$.

**Proof.**

Let $G = \operatorname{Gal}(g/\mathbb{Q})$ act on the $p$ roots $\alpha_1,\dots,\alpha_p$ of $g$ in its splitting field $K \subset \mathbb{C}$. Since $g$ is irreducible of degree $p$, $G$ acts **transitively** on the roots, so $G$ is a transitive subgroup of $S_p$.

**Step 1. $G$ contains a transposition.**

The polynomial $g$ has real (rational) coefficients, so non-real roots occur in conjugate pairs. By hypothesis $g$ has exactly $p-2$ real roots, hence exactly $2$ non-real roots, which must be a conjugate pair $\alpha, \overline{\alpha}$.

Complex conjugation $c : z \mapsto \overline{z}$ is an automorphism of $\mathbb{C}$ fixing $\mathbb{Q}$ pointwise. Since $g$ has real coefficients, $c$ permutes the roots of $g$, hence restricts to an automorphism $c|_K \in \operatorname{Gal}(K/\mathbb{Q}) = G$. As a permutation of the roots, $c$ fixes the $p-2$ real roots and swaps $\alpha \leftrightarrow \overline{\alpha}$. Thus $c|_K$ is a **transposition** in $G$.

**Step 2. A transitive subgroup of $S_p$ ($p$ prime) is primitive.**

For a transitive group of degree $n$, every block in a block system has size dividing $n$ (by orbit–stabilizer counting). With $n = p$ prime, a nontrivial block would have size strictly between $1$ and $p$, which is impossible. Hence $G$ is **primitive**.

**Step 3. (Jordan) A primitive subgroup of $S_n$ containing a transposition equals $S_n$.**

Let $H \le S_n$ be primitive and let $\tau = (a\,b) \in H$. Build a graph $\Gamma$ on $\{1,\dots,n\}$ whose edges are $\{i,j\}$ with $(i\,j) \in H$. If $g \in H$, then $g(i\,j)g^{-1} = (g(i)\,g(j)) \in H$, so $g$ permutes the edges of $\Gamma$ and therefore permutes its connected components $C_1,\dots,C_r$.

This means $\{C_1,\dots,C_r\}$ is an $H$-invariant partition of $\{1,\dots,n\}$, i.e. a block system. Since $H$ is primitive, either $r = 1$ (one component, graph connected) or every $C_k$ is a singleton. But $\tau = (a\,b) \in H$ gives the edge $\{a,b\}$, so the component containing $a$ has size $\ge 2$; thus not all components are singletons. Hence $r = 1$: $\Gamma$ is connected.

A connected graph's edge-transpositions generate $S_n$: indeed if $(i\,j),(j\,k) \in H$ then $(i\,k) = (i\,j)(j\,k)(i\,j) \in H$, so along any path all vertex transpositions lie in $H$, giving all transpositions, hence $H = S_n$.

**Step 4. Conclusion.**

Applying Step 3 with $n = p$: $G$ is primitive (Step 2) and contains a transposition (Step 1), so

$$\boxed{\operatorname{Gal}(g/\mathbb{Q}) \cong S_p.}$$

